    <content>
      <Value><![CDATA[## The Fork in the Road

When creating the Blog Post document type, I had to choose: **Markdown Editor** or **Rich Text Editor** (Tiptap)?

Both are built into Umbraco. Both convert to HTML. But they're fundamentally different.

I chose Markdown. Here's why.

### What Markdown Gives Me

**Simplicity**: Markdown is plain text with formatting hints:
```markdown
## Heading
**bold** and *italic*
- bullet points
```

No visual editor. No WYSIWYG interface. Just text.

**Portability**: Markdown files are universal. They work anywhere—GitHub, VS Code, static site generators, note-taking apps. Content isn't locked into a proprietary format.

**Focus**: When writing Markdown, you think about *content* first, *presentation* second. No fiddling with fonts or colors. Just structured text.

### What I Gave Up

Rich text editors offer:
- Visual formatting (see exactly what it will look like)
- Embedded media (drag and drop images)
- Complex layouts (tables, columns)

For a blog, I don't need any of that. Simple headings, paragraphs, lists, and code blocks are plenty.

### The Technical Win

Umbraco converts Markdown to HTML automatically. In my Razor template:

```csharp
var content = Model.Value<string>("content");
@Html.Raw(content)
```

That's it. No parsing libraries. No custom rendering. Umbraco handles everything.

### The Philosophy

Markdown embodies a philosophy: **content > presentation**.

When I write in Markdown, I'm saying: "Here's my message. The styling will take care of itself."

It's minimalist. It's pragmatic. It's perfect for a blog.

### The Verdict

For this site, Markdown was the right choice. It keeps me focused on writing, not formatting. And in the end, the words matter more than the wrapper.

After all, you're here to read what I have to say—not to admire my font choices.]]></Value>
